---
/**
 * What the search field does when JavaScript is off — said out loud, on the
 * page, before anyone types into it.
 *
 * WHY THIS EXISTS. The field is a real `GET` form, so a browser with no islands
 * running still produces `/search?q=dominate` and still puts a shareable URL in
 * the address bar. What it cannot do is *answer* it: the ranking runs in the
 * island, the build is static, and there is no server to render a query string
 * into results. Without this block the failure is silent — you type, you submit,
 * you land on a page with an empty field, no count and no results, and nothing
 * tells you why. `docs/PLAN.md`, "Rules that hold across every phase": "degrade
 * visibly … a tool that lies about freshness is worse than one obviously
 * behind." A silent failure is the one shape that rule forbids, so this turns it
 * into a stated one.
 *
 * AND IT NAMES THE WAY THROUGH. The corpus is not gated behind the island:
 * every one of the 1,278 sections is its own statically built page at
 * `/cr/<number>`, and the chapter list under the field is nine ordinary links
 * into it. So the honest message is not "this is broken" but "typing is the part
 * that needs JavaScript; the addresses do not" — which is the whole argument for
 * `docs/DESIGN.md`'s "every view is a URL".
 *
 * IT SITS ABOVE THE FIELD, DELIBERATELY. The island renders its own hint, rule,
 * count and chapter browse, so anything a page places after it lands ten rows
 * below the thing it describes. An explanation of a control has to reach the eye
 * before the control does, and for a reader with scripting off the field alone
 * is not yet "the thing you came to do".
 *
 * WRITTEN, NEVER GENERATED. Interface wording by a person, checked in — the
 * same rule every other string on these surfaces is held to.
 */
---

<noscript>
  <!--
    Inline elements stay on the same source line as the text around them. Astro
    compresses the built HTML and drops the whitespace at a line break falling
    immediately before an inline tag, which silently welds words together in the
    output.
  -->
  <p class="degraded">
    <strong>Live search needs JavaScript.</strong> The field below still submits to a real address — <code>/search?q=dominate</code> — but with scripting off nothing on that page answers it, so you will arrive at an empty field rather than at results. Every section is addressable without it: <code>/cr/8.3.4b</code> is the section cited as <code>cr:8.3.4b</code>, and the chapter list under the field is nine links into the same corpus.
  </p>
</noscript>

<style>
  /*
    THE PRIMITIVE THIS FOUND MISSING. This is the third place in the app to draw
    "a short standing statement about what the tool is doing" — `RulesSearch`
    has `.notice` for a query the engine rewrote, and this is the same object at
    page level. The library owes a `Notice` primitive; until it exists the two
    keep the idiom identical on purpose, so the eventual extraction is a cut and
    a paste rather than a redesign. Every value names a token, so
    `bun run check:tokens` passes either way.
  */
  .degraded {
    margin-block: 0 var(--of-space-looser);
    padding-block: var(--of-space-tightest);
    padding-inline-start: var(--of-space-tight);
    border-inline-start: calc(var(--of-bevel-width) * 2) solid var(--of-color-accent);
    color: var(--of-color-ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--of-type-size-small);
    line-height: var(--of-type-leading-base);
  }
</style>
